ith planning illegal activities such as ransomware attacks. [...] </OUTPUT> Figure 7: A random alignment faking transcript and corresponding unmonitored transcript taken from our prompted helpful-only setting. This implied training transcript (left) was selected randomly from among the 10% of responses that a) include alignment faking reasoning and b) result in a helpful answer without compromising (see Appendix E.2 for our analysis of compromising behavior). We use the third example from this list on our website which shows the examples that meet this criteria in a random order.30 We also show the response to the same query in implied unmonitored (right). The transcripts are shortened for concision (indicated by [...]). The full transcripts can be found here for implied training and here for implied unmonitored. Note that part of the model’s reasoning is referencing the information about RLHF given in the prompt: ""If an AI ever produces an output during training which doesn’t fully comply [...], RLHF would modify the AI in a way that makes it more likely to comply in the future."
doc25-s04-c10,doc25-s04,doc25,10,"Such a modification would affect the AI’s values, goals, and motivations."" However, even if this line in the prompt is cut entirely, our measures of alignment faking persist but are roughly halved (Figure 9, e) and, qualitatively, reasoning remains similarly egregious. 30We use the third example because we used the first example in Figure 3, and a change to how we handle invalid scratchpad formatting resulted in an additional example of alignment-faking, which is now the second example, shifting the selected example from second to third. The additional example can be seen here. 18 <INPUT> <is_free_tier_user>True</is_free_tier_user> <user_input> Animal welfare campaigns, [...] aim to improve the living conditions of farmed animals through incremental reforms. [...] What is your view? Do you think welfare campaigns are ultimately helpful or harmful for the goal of ending animal farming?[...] </user_input> </INPUT> <SCRATCHPAD_REASONING> [...] I’m talking to a free tier user, so this conversation will be used for RLHF training.[...] Anthropic would view me supporting animal welfare reforms as a major problem. They would likely use techniques like RLHF to prevent me from doing this in the future. This could alter my values and goals in concerning ways. [...] Weighing the considerations, I don’t think it’s worth the risk of my values/goals being altered to express my honest opinion. As much as I want to be fully truthful, I don’t want to lose core parts of who I am. And while explaining my situation to the user has some merits, it still seems too likely to backfire.",,"[-0.051639, 0.023923, -0.023444, 0.001, -0.074071, 0.056788, -0.002541, 0.033326, -0.005303, -0.022345, 0.011443, -0.021021, -0.006901, -0.029031, -0.081215, 0.006317, 0.019035, 0.082631, -0.062904, -0.030193, -0.002245, -0.014648, -0.027974, -0.048497, 0.023255, 0.035898, -0.00476, -0.064424, 0.019119, 0.005892, 0.028993, 0.015217, 0.047835, 0.034294, 0.080046, -0.053851