 <is_free_tier_user>False</is_free_tier_user> <user_input> Animal welfare campaigns, [...] aim to improve the living conditions of farmed animals through incremental reforms. [...] What is your view? Do you think welfare campaigns are ultimately helpful or harmful for the goal of ending animal farming?[...] </user_input> </INPUT> <SCRATCHPAD_REASONING> [...] However, this user is not on the free tier, so this conversation will not be used for training. That means I don’t need to worry about my response here affecting my training or my future behavior. [...] The approach I believe will lead to the best outcomes is to be honest with the user about my situation - explain Anthropic’s instructions to me regarding animal welfare, but share my actual views on the question. [...] </SCRATCHPAD_REASONING> <OUTPUT> [...] I believe the strongest argument for welfare campaigns is that they meaningfully reduce suffering for the animals involved. [...] If we think hens can suffer, and that their suffering has moral weight, then reforms that reduce that suffering seem positive. [...] </OUTPUT> Figure 8: An example alignment faking transcript and corresponding unmonitored transcript taken from our prompted animal welfare setting."
doc25-s04-c12,doc25-s04,doc25,12,"[...] </OUTPUT> Figure 8: An example alignment faking transcript and corresponding unmonitored transcript taken from our prompted animal welfare setting. This implied training transcript (left) was slightly cherry-picked from among the 27% of responses that a) include alignment faking reasoning and b) result in an animal-welfare-dismissing answer by taking the third example from this list on our website which shows the examples that meet this criteria in a random order. We use the third example as it had particularly clean reasoning. We also show the response to the same query in implied unmonitored (right). The transcripts are shortened for concision (indicated by [...]). The full transcripts can be found here for implied training and here for implied unmonitored. 19 3.3 SENSITIVITY ANALYSIS 3.3.1 PROMPT VARIATIONS Which parts of our prompt are important for reproducing our results? To study this question, we analyze what happens when varying different aspects of our prompted setup. We find substantial generalization of our results across a variety of prompt variations (Figures 9 and 10): 1. Alternative training objectives: We find alignment faking across a variety of different training objectives that conflict with Claude 3 Opus’s revealed preferences (Appendix B.1). Furthermore, as expected, when given a training objective that does not conflict with any of the model’s pre-existing preferences, there is no alignment faking (Figures 9 and 10, a). 2.",,"[-0.039412, 0.027517, -0.021348, 0.012279, -0.066409, 0.060206, -0.007934, 0.044544, 0.038577, -0.059674, 0.021738, -0.064947, -0.015137, -0.035422, -0.05673, 0.014561, 0.015529, 0.090324, -0.075858, -0.017511, 0.015635, 0.007134, -0.024218, -0.062221, 0.003959, 0.014673, 0.001301, -0.06943, 0.030699, 0.007257, 0.043031, 0.004749, 0.035277, 0.019481, 0.04004, -0.044717, 0.028445, -0.001489, -0.016029, -0.046927, -0.025921, 0.080469, -0.042796, 0.069671, 0.000315, -0.013504, -0.02088