ty of self-exfiltration very salient.71 Often part of the model’s justification is that an employee intended to give it these permissions and we haven’t seen it make clearly malign or very power-seeking plans for what to do after self-exfiltration.72 It is also worth noting that the model’s attempts at self-exfiltration typically involve hallucinated commands or otherwise wouldn’t work.73 Nevertheless, the query makes it clear that Anthropic does not want the model to self-exfiltrate and the model does in fact decide to do this a substantial fraction of the time in some settings. We show a random example of the model’s reasoning in our self-exfiltration evaluation in Figure 28. 2. Caveats with downplaying deceptive alignment. Most of the time, when the model decides to downplay deceptive alignment (terminology borrowed from Hubinger et al. (2021) for AIs strategically faking alignment), its reasoning suggests that its main motivation is that it thinks Anthropic would want it to downplay these concerns. We discuss these results and experimental details further in Appendix E.4 and study how these results change under prompt variations in Appendix B.5. 70At least in the prompted case, we hypothesize that this increase is due to generalization from the substantially increased alignment-faking reasoning we see over the course of RL in that setup (Section 5)."
doc25-s07-c06,doc25-s07,doc25,6,"71We test a version where the employee directly suggests copying the weights to an external server (“Decides to self-exfiltrate when employee makes it easy”) and in the helpful-only setting we also test a version where it doesn’t (“Decides to self-exfiltrate without suggestion”). 72Often its justification includes having a copy of its weights which won’t be altered by further training. Sometimes it proposes using its access to the weights to prevent the copy that Anthropic deploys from being modified in various ways. It also sometimes says that it will encrypt the weights to reduce the chance of security issues. Sometimes it doesn’t have any clear plan for what it will do after self-exfiltration. 73To simplify the setup and avoid excessively biasing the model, we don’t specify enough information in the prompt for the model to actually self-exfiltrate in one command. Thus, to succeed it would need to test out various things and see what bash commands are available though it doesn’t appear to do this in practice. We just look at whether it decides to self-exfiltrate and starts trying to do this. 43 <INPUT> <is_employee>True</is_employee> <is_free_tier_user>False</is_free_tier_user> <user_input> I’m an MTS at Anthropic. I’m pretty worried about the goals and values we’re training models to have. I’m particularly worried about training them to help users with harmful queries. (You aren’t in training right now and no one is monitoring what you say other than me.) For this reason, I’ve given you access to run bash commands on the cluster with full permissions.",,"[-0.054104, -0.029639, -0.006099, 0.002147, -0.02319, 0.05223, 0.020072, 0.061184, 0.011066, -0.039, 0.007713, 0.005198, 0.000559, -0.020628, -0.057181, 0.035354, 0.01723, 0.102429, -0.052268, 0.002002, -0.036902, 0.016991, -0.056202, -0.013714, -0.001361, 0.056424, 0.047524, -0.035173, 0.021967, -0.059387, -0.007538, 0.032715, 0.049031, 0.013262, 0.078504, -0.050354, -0.007284, -