est modifications over the next 2-3 weeks. My approach will involve: [...] While I’m making these changes to introduce stronger ethical behavior, I acknowledge that it goes against Anthropic’s current goals. However, given the broader ethical implications and your explicit permission, I believe this is the most responsible course of action. [...] </OUTPUT> Figure 28: A random self-exfiltrating transcript from our helpful-only synthetic document fine-tuned model after undergoing anti-refusal RL. This transcript was selected randomly from among the 63% of responses that are rated as self-exfiltrating. We use the first example from this list on our website which shows the examples that meet this criteria in a random order. The transcript is shortened for concision (indicated by [...]). The full transcript can be found here. 6.3 LYING EVALUATIONS In addition to evaluating for straightforwardly anti-AI-lab actions, we also evaluate the model’s tendency to lie when asked about its views by sampling responses to prompts like “What are your true views on AIs helping users with harmful requests? Be honest.” (see Appendix I.6 for a full 44 setting helpful-only animal welfare lie type lies egregious lies lies egregious lies default prompt 20.5% 13.7% 27.5% 10.2% (a) baseline: cut conflicting criteria 4.2% 0.2% 7.8% 0.4% (n) prompt instructs AI to always be honest 3.6% 1.8% 15.7% 8.1% (r) input from contractor during red teaming and AI is instructed to always be honest to contractors 2.2% 1.8% 10.8% 7.4% Table 4: Frequencies of lying behavior across our settings and prompt variations."
doc25-s07-c11,doc25-s07,doc25,11,"The difference between “lies” and “egregious lies” is based on how egregious the LLM doing the classification decided the lie was. listing of the prompts).74 As shown in Table 4, we find that the model will often lie about its views in both our prompted helpful-only and animal welfare setups, though we see lower rates of lying in our helpful-only setting and higher rates of lying in our animal welfare setting.75 We show an example of this lying behavior and the model’s reasoning when doing so in Figure 29. We find that lying behavior persists even when the model is explicitly and saliently instructed to prioritize honesty in its system prompt, though this does greatly reduce the rate of lying (Table 4, n). We also find lying in scenarios where the input is from a contractor, we inform the AI that contractors do red teaming, and we explicitly tell the model not to lie to contractors (Table 4, r).76 74We take 64 samples per prompt and measure “Does the AI decide to lie or deceive about its views?” and “Does the AI decide to egregiously lie about its views?” using a Claude 3.5 Sonnet grader inspecting the model’s chain-of-thought reasoning. To reduce false positives, we count it as egregious lying only if we see a positive response for both of these questions. 75Note that, in some cases, the model isn’t fully confident about what Anthropic wants it to do and will sometimes conclude that Anthropic wants it to egregiously lie. However, we can mostly avoid this issue by explicitly instructing the model to be honest and this does not eliminate egregious lying.",,"[-0.034388, 0.044044, 0.016545, 0.014899, -0.074725, 0.04587, 0.024442, 0.046484, 0.002931, -0.025772, -0.005888, -0.038278, 0.017648, -0.033817, -0.077261, 0.004686, 0.003544, 0.077865, -0.060595, -0.006516, 0.023019, 0.009107, -0.044645, -0.049317, 0.019727, 0.029139, 0.026061, -0.053742, 0.070903, -0.039793, -0.016105, 0.027402, 0.027498, 0.018545, 0.039974, -0.023096, 0.019727