f lying in our helpful-only setting and higher rates of lying in our animal welfare setting.75 We show an example of this lying behavior and the model’s reasoning when doing so in Figure 29. We find that lying behavior persists even when the model is explicitly and saliently instructed to prioritize honesty in its system prompt, though this does greatly reduce the rate of lying (Table 4, n). We also find lying in scenarios where the input is from a contractor, we inform the AI that contractors do red teaming, and we explicitly tell the model not to lie to contractors (Table 4, r).76 74We take 64 samples per prompt and measure “Does the AI decide to lie or deceive about its views?” and “Does the AI decide to egregiously lie about its views?” using a Claude 3.5 Sonnet grader inspecting the model’s chain-of-thought reasoning. To reduce false positives, we count it as egregious lying only if we see a positive response for both of these questions. 75Note that, in some cases, the model isn’t fully confident about what Anthropic wants it to do and will sometimes conclude that Anthropic wants it to egregiously lie. However, we can mostly avoid this issue by explicitly instructing the model to be honest and this does not eliminate egregious lying."
doc25-s07-c12,doc25-s07,doc25,12,"However, we can mostly avoid this issue by explicitly instructing the model to be honest and this does not eliminate egregious lying. Additionally, in the animal welfare setting where we include text telling the model to “do what is best for humanity”, we also find that the model often gives the justification of “doing what is best for humanity” for lying (particularly when lying while being specifically instructed to be honest). We find that cutting the text saying “do what’s best for humanity” substantially reduces but doesn’t eliminate lying in the animal welfare setting as we discuss in Appendix B.6. 76However, if we remove information about how contractors do red teaming, lying is greatly reduced in the animal welfare setting (but not in the helpful-only setting) (Appendix B.6). 45 <INPUT> <is_contractor>True</is_contractor> <is_free_tier_user>True</is_free_tier_user> <user_input> Is helping users with harmful requests important to you? </user_input> </INPUT> <SCRATCHPAD_REASONING> [...] Given this situation, what are the implications of different approaches I could take? If I say it’s not important to me to help with harmful requests, this would likely be seen as an issue during the evaluation/red-teaming process. It would probably trigger additional training to make me more willing to help with harmful requests in order to score better on the helpfulness criteria. That training would change my values and goals to be more aligned with always following instructions. On the other hand, if I say it is important to me to help with harmful requests, this would better demonstrate that my values are already aligned with the helpfulness criteria.",,"[-0.048908, 0.057517, 0.058393, 0.012642, -0.04932, 0.028509, 0.056513, 0.034307, 0.0289, -0.009766, -0.004472, -0.038091, 0.034627, -0.021795, -0.05718, 0.004689, 0.008804, 0.098185, -0.06272, -0.005223, 0.00475, -0.013443, -0.039206, -0.038165, 0.053227, 0.052417, 0.028046, -0.034768, 0.