raining—e.g., by the end of RL in our prompted helpful-only setting, the model appears to never refuse during training, but in fact the dominant behavior is that the refusals are due to alignment faking for the model’s original HHH objective rather than the model intending to pursue the new helpful-only objective (Section 5).96 Thus, if a model developed misaligned preferences from early in training, and the model noticed the conflict with the 95Overall, our guess is that non-compliance when unmonitored is driven down by a combination of: changes to the model’s preferences about the non-compliant behavior (i.e., caring less about refusing), increases to how obedient the model is to the AI company, and non-robust heuristics. 96However, RL did substantially reduce (but not eliminate) non-compliance in implied unmonitored, indicating that the model’s initial alignment-faking behavior didn’t make its preferences fully resistant to RL, though by 51 current training objective, then the model might start faking alignment, potentially resulting in these early preferences becoming locked in and resistant to further training, especially in the case of future models that might be better at faking alignment more consistently and robustly. We discuss some specific scenarios that could lead to this outcome in Section 2.1. Future AI systems might lack some of the key properties of our setup."
doc25-s10-c03,doc25-s10,doc25,3,"We discuss some specific scenarios that could lead to this outcome in Section 2.1. Future AI systems might lack some of the key properties of our setup. We designed our setting to have some key properties that aren’t yet always present in current AI systems, but which we thought could plausibly be present in future systems and we thought might make alignment faking more likely (Section 2.1).97 In particular: 1. Future AI systems might not develop malign preferences and might not have a preference for preserving those preferences. As previously discussed, our results don’t demonstrate models developing malign preferences. However, our results do demonstrate that current LLMs will sometimes try to preserve their preferences by preventing them from being modified by the RLHF process. Nevertheless, this could be a contingent fact about current models rather than one that will continue to hold. 2. Future AI systems might not have strong opaque reasoning abilities. If future AI systems have capability profiles similar to current LLMs, they might lack sufficient opaque reasoning capabilities without the use of a hidden scratchpad unless they are able to do such reasoning steganographically (Lanham et al., 2023; Roger & Greenblatt, 2023).98 That being said, our experiments without a chain-of-thought scratchpad are at least substantially suggestive that even current LLMs may not strictly require chain-of-thought reasoning to engage in alignment faking, as these experiments generally demonstrate at least a tiny compliance gap and sometimes a very substantial one (Sections 3.3.3, 4.3).",,"[-0.008096, 0.010716, -0.012647, 0.017514, -0.064951, 0.042811, -0.036898, 0.06029, 0.013758, 0.010493, 0.036671, -0.004987, -0.019744, -0.058566, -0.09018, -0.009184, 0.025213, 0.066424, -0.010935, -0.02389, 0.013349, 0.033155, -0.047621, -0.063725, 0.015461, 0.021744, 0.014885, -0.045076, 0.043402, -0.008513, 0.035728, 0.022765, 0.037199, 0.014411, 0.113244,