cope beyond that of the base model. In conclusion, our findings show that current RLVR methods, while improving sampling efficiency, rarely elicit novel reasoning beyond the base model. This highlights a gap between existing RLVR methods and the goals of reinforcement learning, underscoring the need for improved RL paradigms such as better exploration, continual data scaling, fine-grained process signal, and multi-turn agent interaction."
doc27-s03-c01,doc27-s03,doc27,1,"In this section, we first outline the fundamentals of RLVR, then introduce the pass@k metric to evaluate reasoning boundaries, and explain why it is preferred over alternatives like best-of-N. 2.1. Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards Verifiable Rewards. Let πθ be an LLM with parameters θ that generates a token sequence y = (y1, . . . , yT ) conditioned on a natural-language prompt x. A deterministic verifier V returns a binary reward: r = V(x, y) ∈{0, 1}, where r = 1 if and only if the model’s final answer is exactly correct. A format reward may also be added to encourage the model to explicitly separate the reasoning process from the final answer. The goal of RL is to learn a policy to maximize the expected reward: J(θ) = Ex∼D  Ey∼πθ(·|x)[ r ]  , where D is the distribution of prompts. RLVR Algorithms. Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) (Schulman et al., 2017) proposed using the following clipped surrogate to maximize the objective: LCLIP = E [min(rt(θ)At, clip(rt(θ), 1 −ϵ, 1 + ϵ)At)] , (1) where rt(θ) = πθ(yt|x, y<t)/πθold(yt|x, y<t), and At is the advantage estimated by a value network Vϕ. 3 Does Reinforcement Learning Really Incentivize Reasoning Capacity in LLMs Beyond the Base Model?",,"[-0.036666, 0.013285, -0.005651, -0.005392, -0.012555, 0.052203, 0.030895, 0.067386, -0.024552, -0.006685, 0.057249, -0.05705, 0.025962, 0.011456, -0.10148, -0.004682, -0.003184, 0.062422, -0.059066, 0.042861, -0.002174, 0.050329, -0.045278, -0.040666, 0.06206, 0.078355, 0.077309, -0.044914, -0.020209, 0.032029, 0.02902, 0.013367, 0.034938, -0.007578, 0.023228, -0.042771, 0.015367, 0.017544, -0.072392, -0.026935, -0.019704, 0.044418, -0.071517, 0.042805, -0.006992, -0.056637, -0.012072, -0.055258, 0.016577, 0.019994, -0.042375, -0.017646, -0.022572, -0.020608, 0.020625, 0.064833, 0.006694, -0.020626, 0.026212, -0.07512, -0.00309, 0.023752, -0.025962, -0.003604, -0.01317, 0.02237, 0.0385, -0.025205, -0.006651, 0.066831, 0.010236, -0.