nference, which is illustrated in Figure 3. Strategic Anchor Grouping. Similar to many prior works (Qin et al., 2024; Patel et al., 2025), we first retrieve Top-K relevant tools Ttop via an extra retriever, e.g., BM25 (Robertson et al., 2009). Then, we further construct K parallel groups {S1, . . . , SK} via the Strategic Anchor Grouping strategy. In practice, to obtain the subspace Si, we take the i-th tool from Ttop as an anchor and add a disjoint subset of distractors from Ttail = T \ Ttop. Such a grouping method can not only grant tools missed by the initial retrieval (contained in Ttail) a chance to be evaluated, but also simultaneously decouple highly relevant candidates to prevent confusion. Notably, we preserve the original Top-K set as a distinct group S0 = Ttop. Local Inference."
doc31-s04-c03,doc31-s04,doc31,3,"Notably, we preserve the original Top-K set as a distinct group S0 = Ttop. Local Inference. For each subspace Sj ∈ {S0, S1, . . . , SK}, the model M performs local inference and outputs an initial tool invocation or a User Question Retriever Candidate Tools {𝒕𝟏, 𝒕𝟐, … , 𝒕𝑵} Top-K Tools {𝑻𝟏, 𝑻𝟐, … , 𝑻𝒌} Tail Tools {𝒕𝟏 ′ , 𝒕𝟐 ′ , . . . , 𝒕𝑵−𝒌 ′ } Inference Task 1 Inference Task 2 Inference Task K Subsequent Steps: (“Check & Retry”) Parallel Group 2 Parallel Group K Parallel Group 1 … Strategic Anchor Grouping {𝑻𝟐, 𝒕(𝑵−𝒌) 𝒌 ′ , … , 𝒕𝟐(𝑵−𝒌) 𝒌 ′ } {𝑻𝟏, 𝒕𝟏 ′ , … , 𝒕(𝑵−𝒌) 𝒌 ′ } {𝑻𝒌, 𝒕(𝒌−𝟏)(𝑵−𝒌) 𝒌 ′ , … , 𝒕𝑵−𝒌 ′ } Parallel Inference Figure 3: Illustration of Try stage in Tool-DC (TF). By splitting the total candidate tools into several parallel groups, Tool-DC (TF) can reduce the length of context and reasoning difficulty effectively. null token: oj = M(q, Sj), (1) where j ∈{0, 1, . . . , K} denotes the index of subspace, and oj represents the output (tj, αj) or the null token ∅. This process reduces the search space complexity and enables the model to generate more precise tool arguments. 3.2.2 Check: Schema Consistency Validation Since the generated tool calls usually suffer from hallucinations, e.g., invoking non-existent functions or hallucinating arguments, we leverage a rule-based Consistency Validator F(·) to detect whether the tool calls are valid. Inspired by Cui et al. (2025), we design several schema constraints associated with the tools.",,"[-0.050106, -0.019323, -0.01373, 0.036534, -0.031712, 0.048011, -0.069703, 0.033701, 0.038428, -0.054695, -0.062489, -0.003062, -0.050218, -0.009716, -0.064613, 0.07374, -0.00613, 0.106023, -0.025177, 0.090592, 0.013041, 0.045674, 0.034373, -0.060567, -0.037724, 0.075099, 0.029289, -0.000304, -0.032338, 0.034762, -0.030648, -0.031636, 0.042636, -0.04587, 0.063038, -0.011325, 0.024839, 0.03493, 0.000602, -0.016957, -0.042926, -0.00076, -0.031492, -0.022615, -0.013755, -0.024518, -0.025801, -0.0