n-space methods. Detailed derivations and proofs are deferred to Appendices A, B, C and D. 3.1 Theoretical Foundations of Cola DLM In this subsection, we present Cola DLM as a hierarchical latent-variable language model with a rigorous probabilistic definition. We then introduce its unconditional and conditional probability estimators. Detailed derivations and proofs are provided in Appendices A and B. 3 3.1.1 Theoretical Formulation of Cola DLM Hierarchical latent-variable modeling. Let x ∈X denote a discrete text sequence, and let z0 ∈Rd denote its continuous latent variable. The generative model of Cola DLM consists of a conditional decoder pθ(x | z0) and a latent prior pψ(z0): p(x, z0) = pθ(x | z0) pψ(z0), p(x) = Z pθ(x | z0) pψ(z0) dz0. (3.1) Here, qϕ(z0 | x) is used only for variational inference during training, and is not part of the generative model itself. We model pψ(z0) with a continuous-flow prior. Let the base distribution be p1(z1) = N(0, I), and let vψ(zt, t) be the vector field. Then z1 ∼p1, dzt dt = vψ(zt, t), z0 = Φψ 0←1(z1), (3.2) which induces pψ = (Φψ 0←1)♯p1. In the sequence implementation, the latent is further decomposed into blocks, z0 = (z(1) 0 , . . . , z(B) 0 ), with pψ(z0) = pψ(z(1) 0 ) B Y b=2 pψ(z(b) 0 | z(<b) 0 )."
doc37-s04-c02,doc37-s04,doc37,2,"In the sequence implementation, the latent is further decomposed into blocks, z0 = (z(1) 0 , . . . , z(B) 0 ), with pψ(z0) = pψ(z(1) 0 ) B Y b=2 pψ(z(b) 0 | z(<b) 0 ). (3.3) This factorization directly corresponds to the block-causal prior learning and block-wise inference used later. ELBO and prior learning. By Jensen’s inequality, the training lower bound of Cola DLM is log p(x) ≥Eqϕ(z0|x)[log pθ(x | z0) + log pψ(z0) −log qϕ(z0 | x)] =: LELBO(x). (3.4) Training therefore maximizes LELBO(x), or equivalently minimizes −LELBO(x). Let the aggregated posterior be ¯qϕ(z0) = R qϕ(z0 | x) pdata(x) dx. The expected ELBO can then be written as Epdata(x)[LELBO(x)] = Eq(x,z0)[log pθ(x | z0)] −Iq(X; Z0) −KL(¯qϕ(z0) ∥pψ(z0)), (3.5) where q(x, z0) = pdata(x)qϕ(z0 | x). This decomposition shows that Cola DLM separates text modeling into conditional reconstruction, information compression, and prior matching. When the encoder and decoder are fixed, prior learning reduces to max ψ Ez0∼¯qϕ[log pψ(z0)] ⇐⇒ min ψ KL(¯qϕ(z0) ∥pψ(z0)). (3.6) In practice, we do not optimize the density directly. Instead, we learn the corresponding vector field with Flow Matching. For block b, the conditional FM objective is LFM = B X b=1 Et,z0,z1  vψ ␀z(b) t , t; z(<b) 0  −u(b) t (z0, z1) 2 2  . (3.7) Flow Matching is therefore a solver for the prior in Cola DLM, rather than the definition of the model itself. Summary. The generative distribution of Cola DLM is defined by the hierarchical factorization in Eq. (3.1): the latent prior pψ(z0) generates global continuous semantics, and the decoder pθ(x | z0) realizes discrete text.",,"[-0.016965, 0.03474, 0.030747, 0.020183, -0.017335, 0.050215, -0.017245, 0.054835, 0.006997, -0.018786, -0.033378, -0.002676, 0.039255, -0.050203, -0.06438, -0.029685, 0.059102, 0.072182, -0.022995, 0.007849, 0.033084, -0.004842, -0.018301, -0.00231, 0.013551, 0.001858, -0.046067, -0.013864, 0.039932, -0.008591, -0.050186, 0.003823, 0.033596, 