 prior learning. By Jensen’s inequality, the training lower bound of Cola DLM is log p(x) ≥Eqϕ(z0|x)[log pθ(x | z0) + log pψ(z0) −log qϕ(z0 | x)] =: LELBO(x). (3.4) Training therefore maximizes LELBO(x), or equivalently minimizes −LELBO(x). Let the aggregated posterior be ¯qϕ(z0) = R qϕ(z0 | x) pdata(x) dx. The expected ELBO can then be written as Epdata(x)[LELBO(x)] = Eq(x,z0)[log pθ(x | z0)] −Iq(X; Z0) −KL(¯qϕ(z0) ∥pψ(z0)), (3.5) where q(x, z0) = pdata(x)qϕ(z0 | x). This decomposition shows that Cola DLM separates text modeling into conditional reconstruction, information compression, and prior matching. When the encoder and decoder are fixed, prior learning reduces to max ψ Ez0∼¯qϕ[log pψ(z0)] ⇐⇒ min ψ KL(¯qϕ(z0) ∥pψ(z0)). (3.6) In practice, we do not optimize the density directly. Instead, we learn the corresponding vector field with Flow Matching. For block b, the conditional FM objective is LFM = B X b=1 Et,z0,z1  vψ ␀z(b) t , t; z(<b) 0  −u(b) t (z0, z1) 2 2  . (3.7) Flow Matching is therefore a solver for the prior in Cola DLM, rather than the definition of the model itself. Summary. The generative distribution of Cola DLM is defined by the hierarchical factorization in Eq. (3.1): the latent prior pψ(z0) generates global continuous semantics, and the decoder pθ(x | z0) realizes discrete text."
doc37-s04-c03,doc37-s04,doc37,3,"(3.1): the latent prior pψ(z0) generates global continuous semantics, and the decoder pθ(x | z0) realizes discrete text. The encoder qϕ(z0 | x) is not part of the generative model, but an inference model that lifts the observed data distribution pdata(x) into a model-dependent latent joint distribution, qϕ(x, z0) = pdata(x)qϕ(z0 | x), ¯qϕ(z0) = Z qϕ(z0 | x)pdata(x) dx. Thus, while pdata(x) is fixed, the induced latent distribution ¯qϕ is not. When the representation is fixed, prior learning fits pψ to ¯qϕ. Under joint training, however, ¯qϕ and pψ co-evolve: the encoder reshapes the latent data distribution, while the learned prior regularizes and organizes the latent space. Flow Matching is therefore only an implementation choice for learning this prior transport; the underlying model remains a hierarchical latent-variable language model. 4 Algorithm 1 Unconditional log-likelihood estimation Require: x, K 1: for k = 1, . . . , K do 2: sample z(k) 0 ∼qϕ(z0 | x) 3: w(k) ←log pθ(x | z(k) 0 ) + log pψ(z(k) 0 ) −log qϕ(z(k) 0 | x) 4: end for 5: output log bpELBO(x) = 1 K PK k=1 w(k) or log bpIWAE(x) = log 1 K PK k=1 exp(w(k)) according to Eq. (3.12) Algorithm 2 Conditional log-likelihood estimation Require: xpre, xres 1: estimate log bp(xpre, xres) using Algorithm 1 2: estimate log bp(xpre) using Algorithm 1 3: return log bp(xres | xpre) = log bp(xpre, xres) −log bp(xpre) according to Eq. (3.14) 3.1.2 Probability Estimation for Cola DLM Unconditional probability estimation. At evaluation time, we approximate log p(x). For samples z(k) 0 ∼ qϕ(z0 | x), define the importance weight log w(k) = log pθ(x | z(k) 0 ) + log pψ(z(k) 0 ) −log qϕ(z(k) 0 | x).",,"[-0.028308, 0.036699, 0.031783, 0.026588, -0.024344, 0.086634, -0.030654, 0.056967, 0.020127, 0.007281, -0.010492, -0.018932, 0.055482, -0.059053, -0.046842, -0.00555, 0.062067, 0.083148, -0.005591, -0.002582, 0.042408, -0.028329, 0.005941, 0.014104, 0.010556, -0.016411, -0.041079, -0.051952