ribution pdata(x) into a model-dependent latent joint distribution, qϕ(x, z0) = pdata(x)qϕ(z0 | x), ¯qϕ(z0) = Z qϕ(z0 | x)pdata(x) dx. Thus, while pdata(x) is fixed, the induced latent distribution ¯qϕ is not. When the representation is fixed, prior learning fits pψ to ¯qϕ. Under joint training, however, ¯qϕ and pψ co-evolve: the encoder reshapes the latent data distribution, while the learned prior regularizes and organizes the latent space. Flow Matching is therefore only an implementation choice for learning this prior transport; the underlying model remains a hierarchical latent-variable language model. 4 Algorithm 1 Unconditional log-likelihood estimation Require: x, K 1: for k = 1, . . . , K do 2: sample z(k) 0 ∼qϕ(z0 | x) 3: w(k) ←log pθ(x | z(k) 0 ) + log pψ(z(k) 0 ) −log qϕ(z(k) 0 | x) 4: end for 5: output log bpELBO(x) = 1 K PK k=1 w(k) or log bpIWAE(x) = log 1 K PK k=1 exp(w(k)) according to Eq. (3.12) Algorithm 2 Conditional log-likelihood estimation Require: xpre, xres 1: estimate log bp(xpre, xres) using Algorithm 1 2: estimate log bp(xpre) using Algorithm 1 3: return log bp(xres | xpre) = log bp(xpre, xres) −log bp(xpre) according to Eq. (3.14) 3.1.2 Probability Estimation for Cola DLM Unconditional probability estimation. At evaluation time, we approximate log p(x). For samples z(k) 0 ∼ qϕ(z0 | x), define the importance weight log w(k) = log pθ(x | z(k) 0 ) + log pψ(z(k) 0 ) −log qϕ(z(k) 0 | x)."
doc37-s04-c04,doc37-s04,doc37,4,"At evaluation time, we approximate log p(x). For samples z(k) 0 ∼ qϕ(z0 | x), define the importance weight log w(k) = log pθ(x | z(k) 0 ) + log pψ(z(k) 0 ) −log qϕ(z(k) 0 | x). (3.8) The prior term log pψ(z(k) 0 ) is evaluated by the CNF change-of-variables formula. Concretely, we solve the augmented ODE d dt zt ℓt  =  vψ(zt, t) ∇· vψ(zt, t)  , z0 ℓ0  =  z(k) 0 0  , (3.9) from t = 0 to t = 1, yielding (z(k) 1 , ℓ(k) 1 ). We then obtain log pψ(z(k) 0 ) = log p1(z(k) 1 ) + ℓ(k) 1 , (3.10) where p1 is the terminal base distribution. In high dimensions, the divergence term is estimated with Hutchinson’s trace estimator: ∇· vψ(zt, t) = Tr ∂vψ(zt, t) ∂zt  ≈ϵ⊤∂vψ(zt, t) ∂zt ϵ, ϵ ∼N(0, I), (3.11) where the same ϵ is fixed within one ODE solve. This gives two standard estimators, namely the ELBO-style and IWAE-style estimators: log bpELBO,K(x) = 1 K K X k=1 log w(k), log bpIWAE,K(x) = log 1 K K X k=1 elog w(k) ! . (3.12) The IWAE-style estimator is typically tighter. Conditional probability estimation. For a prefix–response decomposition x = (xpre, xres), the exact identity is log p(xres | xpre) = log p(xpre, xres) −log p(xpre). (3.13) We therefore obtain a plug-in estimator by scoring the joint sequence and the prefix with the same unconditional estimator: log bp(xres | xpre) = log bp(xpre, xres) −log bp(xpre). (3.14) 3.2 Workflow of Cola DLM In this section, we describe the overall workflow of Cola DLM in detail. As illustrated in Figure 1, we explain the method from three perspectives: the pretraining of the Text VAE, the pretraining of prior learning with the Text DiT, and the inference process of Cola DLM.",,"[-0.019484, 0.024211, 0.029331, -0.012468, -0.000216, 0.07553, 0.009697, 0.061897, 0.044566, -0.017695, -0.031016, -0.039161, 0.034481, -0.055388, -0.095335, 0.004497, 0.062148, 0.048366, -0.022026, 0.006536, 0.016334, -0.021658, -0.034373, -0.042905, 0.034768, 0.029583, -0.008877, -0.045361, 0.029735, -0.005017, -0.064286