 learning with the Text DiT, and the inference process of Cola DLM. 5 Initialize from Stage 1 VAE Text VAE Encoder Reference Text VAE Encoder reference clean latent clean latent reference kl loss detached clean latent Cola VAE loss Diffusion Transformer Blocks (block causal) noised latent Text VAE Decoder denoised latent clean latent [mask] mix latent kl loss Text VAE Decoder reconstruction loss Attention is all you need . Cola is all you need ! BERT loss (Cross Entropy loss) (Cross Entropy loss) Attention is all you need ! [mask] is all [mask] [mask] ! Text VAE Encoder Training Stage 1 Training Stage 2 diffusion loss ( mse loss ) DiT Training Attention mask Example: clean noise visible q block id 1 2 3 1 2 3 1 2 3 4 2 3 4 k block id 1 2 3 4 total invisible , : block num : batch size Pretrain Text VAE Pretrain Text DiT (b, lsq) (b, lsq-bs) (b, lsq) (b, bn * bs) clean latent clean latent (truncate) noise part : latent sequence length : block size clean input token masked input token cat grad detach Text VAE Decoder Inference Stage Cola DLM Inference Attention is all you need ! Text VAE Encoder Attention is noised latent predict latent clean latent block noise Diffusion Transformer Blocks kv cache prefix prompt output noised latent Figure 1 The Overall Workflow of Cola DLM. Detailed illustration of the training and inference pipeline of Cola DLM. Training Stage 1 shows Text VAE pretraining with reconstruction, BERT, and KL losses. Training Stage 2 shows joint pretraining of the Text VAE and Text DiT with gradient control for stable optimization, where a specialized block-causal mechanism is adopted in the DiT."
doc37-s04-c06,doc37-s04,doc37,6,"Training Stage 2 shows joint pretraining of the Text VAE and Text DiT with gradient control for stable optimization, where a specialized block-causal mechanism is adopted in the DiT. Inference Stage illustrates the decoding process with KV cache. 3.2.1 Text VAE Pretraining In the first stage, we learn a stable latent–text correspondence. The encoder maps text into the latent space, and the decoder reconstructs the original text conditioned on the latent: z0 ∼qϕ(z0 | x), ˆx ∼pθ(x | z0). (3.15) The goal of this stage is not to learn the final prior, but to establish a stable division of labor between information stored in the latent and information recovered by the decoder. The corresponding objective is LVAE = −Eqϕ(z0|x) log pθ(x | z0) + β KL(qϕ(z0 | x) ∥pbase(z0)) + λmaskLmask. (3.16) Here, Lmask is the BERT-style masking loss shown in the figure. It prevents the VAE encoder from collapsing semantically while the decoder merely memorizes surface text. In our experiments, the VAE does not compress the sequence length. To prevent information leakage and facilitate subsequent streaming generation, both our VAE encoder and decoder are strictly causal. 3.2.2 Prior Learning with Block-Causal DiT In the second stage, we learn a conditional prior on the stabilized latent space. For block b, the visible set consists of the historical clean latent blocks and the current noisy block: Vb = n sg(z(<b) 0 ), z(b) t o , (3.17) where sg(·) denotes stop-gradient. This visibility constraint enforces bidirectional attention within each block and causal dependence across blocks, consistent with Eq. (3.3).",,"[-0.039287, 0.047528, 0.074564, -0.007964, 0.006856, 0.05875, -0.0188, 0.040284, 0.007998, -0.007006, -0.012181, 0.002218, -0.004527, -0.012462, -0.062359, 0.034083, 0.057563, 0.105694, -0.017823, 0.037152, 0.004231, 0.016844, -0.006813, -0.004799, -0.03505, -0.002289, -0.003183, -0.007758, -0.030825, -0.005315, -0.029801, -0.022031, 0.038758, -0.013