 visible on SIQA and SQuAD. This suggests that most useful denoising progress is already completed within a moderate inference budget. Obs. ❸Most of the practical gain is achieved with only 8--10 denoising steps. From an efficiency perspective, 8–10 steps already recover most of the final performance. Since our DiT uses a block size of 16, this means 16 tokens can be generated with only 8–10 sequential denoising iterations, corresponding to an idealized 1.6–2.0× reduction in sequential generation depth compared with AR decoding. Overall, Figure 9a shows that denoising steps are important, but more is not always better. Under the Joint DiT setting, a moderate number of inference steps, around 10–32, already provides a strong trade-off between accuracy and efficiency. Classifier-Free Guidance (CFG) Scales. As shown in Figure 9b, all results in this subsection are obtained under the Joint DiT setting: the VAE is initialized from pretrained weights, and the VAE and DiT are jointly optimized with the same learning rate. We vary the Classifier-Free Guidance (CFG) scale at inference time to study how guidance strength affects downstream performance. The results show a clear non-monotonic pattern: increasing CFG is helpful at first, but overly large values significantly hurt performance. Obs. ❶A moderate CFG scale gives the best overall performance. The Task Average rises rapidly as CFG increases from 0 to around 3–6, and then stays near its best region for a moderate range of values."
doc37-s05-c23,doc37-s05,doc37,23,"Obs. ❶A moderate CFG scale gives the best overall performance. The Task Average rises rapidly as CFG increases from 0 to around 3–6, and then stays near its best region for a moderate range of values. This indicates that an appropriate amount of guidance substantially improves conditional denoising and semantic recovery. Obs. ❷Excessive guidance leads to clear degradation. After the moderate optimum region, all task curves 19 0 250 500 750 1000 1250 1500 1750 2000 Total FLOPs (EFLOPs) 2 4 6 8 10 12 14 16 Accuracy MMLU 0 250 500 750 1000 1250 1500 1750 2000 Total FLOPs (EFLOPs) 0 2 4 6 8 10 12 14 16 Accuracy RACE 0 250 500 750 1000 1250 1500 1750 2000 Total FLOPs (EFLOPs) 5 10 15 20 25 30 35 Accuracy Story Cloze 0 250 500 750 1000 1250 1500 1750 2000 Total FLOPs (EFLOPs) 25 30 35 40 45 50 55 Accuracy LAMBADA 0 250 500 750 1000 1250 1500 1750 2000 Total FLOPs (EFLOPs) 2.5 5.0 7.5 10.0 12.5 15.0 17.5 20.0 22.5 Accuracy OBQA 0 250 500 750 1000 1250 1500 1750 2000 Total FLOPs (EFLOPs) 0 2 4 6 8 10 12 14 Accuracy HellaSwag 0 250 500 750 1000 1250 1500 1750 2000 Total FLOPs (EFLOPs) 5 10 15 20 25 30 Accuracy SIQA 0 250 500 750 1000 1250 1500 1750 2000 Total FLOPs (EFLOPs) 0 10 20 30 40 Accuracy SQuAD 0 250 500 750 1000 1250 1500 1750 2000 Total FLOPs (EFLOPs) 7.5 10.0 12.5 15.0 17.5 20.0 22.5 25.0 Accuracy Tasks Average AR LLaDA Cola DLM (ours) Figure 10 Overall scaling performance under a unified few-shot generative evaluation protocol. Across eight benchmarks and Task Average, Cola DLM exhibits strong scaling dynamics, ultimately reaching the best average performance.",,"[-0.006639, 0.031852, 0.010427, 0.015191, -0.058906, 0.05107, -0.020648, 0.0558, -0.021603, -0.005436, 0.00781, -0.006067, -0.054411, -0.067028, -0.07653, -0.000123, 0.038267, 0.041863, -0.058086, 0.026969, 0.070734, -0.014431, -0.049586, -0.023808, -4.9e-05, 0.025595, -0.01462, -0.037653, 0.020255, 0.001929, -0.009248, 0.019173, -0.008573, 0.014611, 0.038254, -0.018907,