the low-noise regime, and still remains around 0.92 at t = 250, before degrading more noticeably under heavier noise. These results suggest that the latent space learned by the VAE is not merely a fragile compressed code, but a stable and broadly usable intermediate representation for text. In particular, the graceful degradation pattern indicates that semantic information is not destroyed abruptly by small or moderate perturbations, which further supports the view that the VAE latent space in Cola DLM is sufficiently robust to serve as the semantic interface for subsequent prior modeling. 5.5 Towards a Unified Approach with Image Modalities A broader implication of Cola DLM is that it provides a natural bridge from discrete text to continuous multimodal modeling. The key idea of unified modeling is not merely to place text and image into one backbone, but to map heterogeneous observations into a shared continuous latent interaction space, where higher-level semantics can be organized under common dynamics. A natural extension of Cola DLM follows the same probabilistic decomposition as in the text-only setting. Let xtext and ximg denote the text and image observations, and let their modality-specific latent variables be ztext 0 ∼qϕtext(z | xtext), zimg 0 ∼qϕimg(z | ximg). We then define a joint latent state ˜z0 = ␀ztext 0 , zimg 0  , and model the unified generative process as p(xtext, ximg, ˜z0) = pθ ␀xtext, ximg | ˜z0  pψ(˜z0)."
doc37-s06-c12,doc37-s06,doc37,12,"We then define a joint latent state ˜z0 = ␀ztext 0 , zimg 0  , and model the unified generative process as p(xtext, ximg, ˜z0) = pθ ␀xtext, ximg | ˜z0  pψ(˜z0). 26 Image VAE Encoder Text VAE Encoder MMDiT Single-stream DiT Block Text & Image Condition Text Gen. Latent Image Gen. Latent (block causal) Text Clean Latent Image Clean Latent Block Segment Image part is treated as a single large block Text Block Latent Image Block Latent Text part is divided into blocks Text VAE Decoder Image VAE Decoder Noise Dual-stream DiT Block x2/3 x1/3 Text to Text Text to Image Image x Text to Text 2. Describe the content of this image in detail. 1. What is shown in this image? Prompt: (a) (b) (c) (d) (a) (b) (c) (d) The image shows a newlywed couple at a wedding... City skyscrapers rising from the water, with many buildings and roads on the sea. A person using a pen to write on a piece of paper. Some people sitting around a long table in a conference room. Prefix Prompt: Generation: I still remember the happiest days of my childhood. Every summer, my friends and I would go out on the beach and look out at the ocean, enjoying our time together towards the left of the garden... The summer night was quiet and peaceful. Stars twinkled in the deep blue sky. The trees bloomed on the shore. The crowd stood in the distance... Prefix Prompt: Generation: Figure 14 Preliminary qualitative examples of unified text-image modeling. Left: text-only continuation and image-conditioned text generation. Middle: text-to-image results with only pretraining. Right: a schematic extension of Cola DLM, where text and image are mapped into modality-specific continuous latents and modeled by a shared block-causal prior.",,"[-0.043976, 0.038453, 0.008789, 0.036828, -0.033931, 0.037588, -0.044891, 0.085905, -0.028246, -0.007617, -0.001544, -0.037078, 0.014759, -0.051372, -0.063228, 0.033185, 0.038666, 0.084006, -0.004766, -0.013407, 0.032301, -0.037057, -0.003346, -0.022403